<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="rutin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Rutin is a flavonol glucoside that is widespread in the plant kingdom, and has been especially found in the |FRAME:TAX-3615| and |FRAME:TAX-3803| families. Rutin has several interesting pharmacological properties (e.g. antioxidative activity) that have been exploited in human medicine and nutrition. Quercetin-3-rhamnoside and its rutinoside, i.e. rutin has been demonstrated to possess antimalarial activity |CITS: [3081917]|. Rutin was identified as one of the most active agents of an aqueous crude extract of &lt;i&gt;Baccharis trimera&lt;/i&gt; that was shown to express strong anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties |CITS: [8693035]|. The inhibitory effect against non-enzymatic lipid peroxidation, deoxyribose degradation |CITS: [9741300]| and on lipopolysaccharide-induced NO production |CITS: [12098601]| emphasizes the biological relevance of rutin in the treatment of related human conditions. Moreover, |FRAME:RUTIN| has been shown to be a promising agent against Alzheimer&apos;s disease |CITS:[22445961]| |CITS:[22441036]|, provides resistance to biotrophic and necrotrophic pathogens |CITS:[22677447]| and showed protective effect against pro-carcinogenic agents |CITS:[20399630]|. Rutin production in plants has been found to be increased under the influence of UV-B radiation. Ambient radiation was the most effective mean to elevate rutin content, especially in leaves, indicating a possible involvement into UV protection |CITS: [12147730]|. The high accumulation of |FRAME:RUTIN| in the upper epidermis of leaves in |FRAME:TAX-3616| species also supports its possible role as UV screen |CITS:[Margna90]|. Rutin content in plants also seems to be influenced by the photoperiod as it was promoted under far red (FR) conditions with no obvious changes in the overall production of phenolics |CITS: [5423471]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This pathway mostly describes the metabolic situation found in buckwheat species and represents a branch of the phenylpropanoid biosynthesis (see also |FRAME:PWY1F-FLAVSYN|, |FRAME:PWY-3101| and |FRAME:PWY-5321|). This has been demonstrated through monitoring the differential expression of involved genes during developmental stages of buckwheat which are characterized by the accumulation of |FRAME:RUTIN| as one of the most abundant flavonol glycoside in this plant |CITS:[21872967]| |CITS:[21062042]|. The increased production of |FRAME:RUTIN| after overexpressing the flavonol-specific transcription factor AtMYB12 in |FRAME:TAX-3617| and observing the expression of genes presumably involved in |FRAME:RUTIN| biosynthesis shows that key enzymes of the phenylpropanoid and flavonoid biosynthetic pathways up to |FRAME:PWY1F-467| are channeled towards |FRAME:RUTIN| formation |CITS:[22113884]|. An important metabolic step in this pathway is carried out by the flavonol synthase catalyzing the |FRAME:2-KETOGLUTARATE| dependent oxygenation of |FRAME:CPD-474| to form |FRAME:CPD-520|. The corresponding gene has been cloned, heterologously expressed and characterized with regard to protein structure and catalytic properties |CITS:[22563787]|. The following conversion in the pathway forming the immediate precursor for rutin, |FRAME:CPD1F-437| (isoquercetin) is realized by the flavonol 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyltransferase |CITS:[Suzuki05]| |CITS:[Lucci09]| (compare also |FRAME:PWY-5321|). The enzyme catalyzing the final step in the pathway, i.e. flavonol-3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside &lt;i&gt;L&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase has been partially purified from mung bean |CITS: [13864833]| and |FRAME:TAX-149691| (fava d&apos;anta) |CITS:[Lucci09]|. The enzyme catalyzes the transfer of a rhamnosyl group from |FRAME:UDP-L-RHAMNOSE| to quercetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside forming rutin. Although dTDP-rhamnose had been considered as the primary donor |CITS: [13864833]| it has been demonstrated that UDP-&lt;i&gt;L&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnose is the native donor for the transfer of &lt;i&gt;L&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnose |CITS: [1898019]| |CITS: [14084592]|. It had long been a pending question how exactly UDP-L-rhamnose was biosynthesized in plants. It is clear now that UDP-&lt;i&gt;L&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnose can be catalyzed from UDP-&lt;i&gt;D&lt;/i&gt;-glucose (see |FRAME:PWY-3261|) |CITS: [1898019]| |CITS: [14084592]| which is an ubiquitous activated nucleotide sugar readily available for metabolic processes in plants.</Description>
